# Doubly Linked List — Basics

## 1. Node Structure

A Doubly Linked List node contains:
- data → stores the value
- next → reference to the next node
- prev → reference to the previous node

class Node {
    int data;
    Node prev;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.prev = null;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

Structure:

null <- 10 <-> 20 <-> 30 -> null


## 2. Traversal

Forward traversal:

Node temp = head;

while (temp != null) {
    System.out.println(temp.data);
    temp = temp.next;
}


Backward traversal is possible because every node has a prev reference.


## 3. Insert Node Before Head

Create a new node.

newNode.next = head;

If the current head exists, update its prev:

if (head != null) {
    head.prev = newNode;
}

Make the new node the head:

head = newNode;

Example:

Before:
10 <-> 20 <-> 30

Insert 5:

5 <-> 10 <-> 20 <-> 30


Important links:

newNode.next = old head
old head.prev = newNode

Time: O(1)


## 4. Delete Head

Move head to the next node:

head = head.next;

Then remove the backward reference:

if (head != null) {
    head.prev = null;
}

Example:

Before:
10 <-> 20 <-> 30

After deleting head:

20 <-> 30


Important:

Always check head != null before accessing head.prev.

Time: O(1)


## Key Difference Between Singly and Doubly Linked List

Singly Linked List:

current -> next

Doubly Linked List:

prev <- current -> next

In DLL, whenever we modify links, we generally need to maintain BOTH prev and next references.


Operations:

Insert Before Head → O(1)
Delete Head → O(1)
Traversal → O(n)